Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.443 --> 00:00:02.530
- Good morning Believe
Nation, today's message is

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:04.943
"be a great leader."

00:00:04.943 --> 00:00:07.110
Over to you, John Addison.

00:00:08.697 --> 00:00:11.415
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:11.415 --> 00:00:14.243
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:14.243 --> 00:00:17.743
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.906
- Weather veins typically are
politicians, where whatever

00:00:22.906 --> 00:00:26.564
way the wind's blowing or
whatever the mood of the

00:00:26.564 --> 00:00:30.731
moment is, whatever the polls
say, they spin that direction.

00:00:31.722 --> 00:00:35.664
Leaders are lighthouses,
there's a consistency to it,

00:00:35.664 --> 00:00:37.430
there's a strength to it.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:41.102
Lighthouses are only needed
when the weather's stormy,

00:00:41.102 --> 00:00:44.041
when it's beautiful they're
just a pretty building.

00:00:44.041 --> 00:00:48.446
But when the seas are stormy
and it's dark and rainy

00:00:48.446 --> 00:00:51.174
and foggy, that's when
you need a lighthouse,

00:00:51.174 --> 00:00:53.341
a leader has to be a rock.

00:00:54.714 --> 00:00:57.177
Someone of consistency with
the storms whether it's

00:00:57.177 --> 00:01:01.380
in business, the economy,
whatever's happening to

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.373
the organization that you're
over, that you're a human

00:01:05.373 --> 00:01:09.424
Alka Seltzer, you calm the organization.

00:01:09.424 --> 00:01:13.175
I've worked for people
that were quote en quote

00:01:13.175 --> 00:01:16.565
leaders, who would bring
a bucket of gas to a fire

00:01:16.565 --> 00:01:19.810
and make it just turn
it into a blaze by how

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.213
they reacted to it.

00:01:22.213 --> 00:01:26.743
Great leaders are calmers,
great leaders are people that

00:01:26.743 --> 00:01:30.883
when maybe everything is
hectic and chaotic, but when

00:01:30.883 --> 00:01:35.527
people see them, hear
from them, they're calmer.

00:01:35.527 --> 00:01:38.539
I believe a peaceful mind generates power.

00:01:38.539 --> 00:01:40.192
- If you want to do something
great then you're going to

00:01:40.192 --> 00:01:41.654
need to build a team,
whether it's in business,

00:01:41.654 --> 00:01:43.874
whether it's some other
movement that you want to build,

00:01:43.874 --> 00:01:46.311
if you want to do something
great, you can't do it alone,

00:01:46.311 --> 00:01:47.914
you need a team!

00:01:47.914 --> 00:01:50.469
And so many people go through
this as they're building

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:53.707
their business, often
they hire the wrong person

00:01:53.707 --> 00:01:56.254
at the start and it doesn't
work out, and they say

00:01:56.254 --> 00:01:58.100
"you know what, forget this,
I'm just going to go back

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.570
"to doing everything myself!"

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:01.407
And that is not the answer.

00:02:01.407 --> 00:02:03.900
The answer is to learn
how to be a better leader.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.365
As your organization gets
bigger and bigger and

00:02:06.365 --> 00:02:10.064
bigger and bigger, your
role, the contribution you're

00:02:10.064 --> 00:02:12.096
giving actually diminishes.

00:02:12.096 --> 00:02:14.977
If you have 100 people in
your company, the thing that

00:02:14.977 --> 00:02:18.567
you're doing day in and day
out is minor compared to what

00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:21.895
your 100 other people are doing for you.

00:02:21.895 --> 00:02:23.845
When you're solo you're
doing everything, when you

00:02:23.845 --> 00:02:26.661
bring on one person maybe
you're doing still 75%

00:02:26.661 --> 00:02:29.500
of the value because you're
the best at what you do,

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.010
but then as you start
bringing more people on,

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:35.347
your contribution diminishes,
and so yes you're focusing

00:02:35.347 --> 00:02:39.076
on what you can become
the best in the world at,

00:02:39.076 --> 00:02:41.919
but your greatest contribution
is going to be leading

00:02:41.919 --> 00:02:44.449
your team to help unlock
their potential and allowing

00:02:44.449 --> 00:02:47.481
them to be the best in
the world at what they do.

00:02:47.481 --> 00:02:50.183
We don't go to school for
leadership, we don't really learn

00:02:50.183 --> 00:02:52.489
leadership at high school,
we don't learn leadership

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:54.371
at university, there's
no course you can take,

00:02:54.371 --> 00:02:57.671
maybe you join a sports team
and maybe you had a coach

00:02:57.671 --> 00:02:59.975
who was amazing who guided
you, but this isn't part

00:02:59.975 --> 00:03:01.982
of the process for most people.

00:03:01.982 --> 00:03:04.416
Most people aren't trained in leadership.

00:03:04.416 --> 00:03:06.175
This is why I think
understanding your one word

00:03:06.175 --> 00:03:08.195
is really important, when
you know what it is that you

00:03:08.195 --> 00:03:11.601
stand for, then it's way
easier to lead a group of

00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:14.337
people, it's way easier to
build a team of people who are

00:03:14.337 --> 00:03:16.653
aligned with what you're trying to do.

00:03:16.653 --> 00:03:19.239
I believe great leadership
is about unlocking the

00:03:19.239 --> 00:03:21.238
potential in the people on your team.

00:03:21.238 --> 00:03:23.544
They're working on a
certain standard which may

00:03:23.544 --> 00:03:25.038
already be high but you
see them being able to

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:26.141
work up here.

00:03:26.141 --> 00:03:28.531
And your job is to help
them close that gap through

00:03:28.531 --> 00:03:31.055
believing in them, through pushing them,

00:03:31.055 --> 00:03:32.900
through training them,
you have to help them

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:34.290
close that gap, that's what
I believe a great leader

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.242
does and the way to do
it, the easiest way in my

00:03:37.242 --> 00:03:39.848
experience is through
understanding their one work

00:03:39.848 --> 00:03:41.706
in their credo.

00:03:41.706 --> 00:03:42.905
Understanding what drives them.

00:03:42.905 --> 00:03:45.731
They are giving you the
tools to help push them.

00:03:45.731 --> 00:03:49.135
So for example somebody
really values creativity,

00:03:49.135 --> 00:03:52.385
versus somebody who really values grit.

00:03:53.244 --> 00:03:55.952
Hard work and grit versus
creativity, those don't have

00:03:55.952 --> 00:04:00.002
to be opposites, but it's
something that they really value.

00:04:00.002 --> 00:04:02.160
And so yes we have
organizational goals, here's what

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.467
we're trying to reach, I
want to help entrepreneurs,

00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:05.879
I want to get to one million
subscribers, that's our next

00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:08.582
goal, we're aligned around that.

00:04:08.582 --> 00:04:11.931
But the people on my team, how
do we go and accomplish it?

00:04:11.931 --> 00:04:13.957
If somebody really values
creativity then I need to push

00:04:13.957 --> 00:04:17.581
them on creativity, I need
more creative solutions.

00:04:17.581 --> 00:04:20.978
I need creative editing, I
need creative videographing.

00:04:20.978 --> 00:04:22.096
Is that a word?

00:04:22.096 --> 00:04:26.388
I need creative thumbnails,
I need more creativity.

00:04:26.388 --> 00:04:29.485
Where if somebody's word is
grit or part of their credo

00:04:29.485 --> 00:04:32.240
was grit, then I need them to work harder.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.427
I need them to push themselves.

00:04:34.427 --> 00:04:36.502
If somebody's word is
"extraordinary," then how can we make

00:04:36.502 --> 00:04:38.489
this more extraordinary?

00:04:38.489 --> 00:04:40.299
What do we need to do, I
need extraordinary thumbnails

00:04:40.299 --> 00:04:42.774
I need extraordinary videography,

00:04:42.774 --> 00:04:44.690
I need extraordinary editing.

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:47.314
I can't make somebody who
values creativity and push

00:04:47.314 --> 00:04:51.114
them to extraordinary,
it's just not who they are,

00:04:51.114 --> 00:04:53.281
there's going to be a disconnect.

00:04:53.281 --> 00:04:56.298
So understanding what our
goal is, having a common goal

00:04:56.298 --> 00:05:00.089
and then giving people the
flexibility and freedom

00:05:00.089 --> 00:05:02.987
to help you accomplish that
goal in a way that best suits

00:05:02.987 --> 00:05:06.592
them and you understanding
them, their one word,

00:05:06.592 --> 00:05:08.565
their credo, they're giving
you the tools to push them.

00:05:08.565 --> 00:05:12.893
They're giving you the tools
to close that gap, between

00:05:12.893 --> 00:05:16.218
where they're at now and
where their potential is

00:05:16.218 --> 00:05:19.046
which in my view is the
ultimate goal of a leader.

00:05:19.046 --> 00:05:23.617
So the question today is I'm
curious, are you a leader

00:05:23.617 --> 00:05:25.579
and how many people are on your team?

00:05:25.579 --> 00:05:27.341
Could be full time, could
be contractors, I'm curious

00:05:27.341 --> 00:05:29.968
how many people are on your
team right now, leave in

00:05:29.968 --> 00:05:32.330
the comments below, super
curious to find out.

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:34.458
I also want to give a quick
shoutout to Daniel Burgess,

00:05:34.458 --> 00:05:36.780
Daniel thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.465
Your One Word, really really
means a lot to me man,

00:05:39.465 --> 00:05:41.059
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:05:41.059 --> 00:05:43.437
So thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you,

00:05:43.437 --> 00:05:46.930
I hope you continue to believe
in yourself, and whatever

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:48.714
your one word is.

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:50.291
I'll see you guys again
tomorrow morning for another

00:05:50.291 --> 00:05:51.874
show of Entspresso.

